    "engine": "jupyter",
    "markdown": "---\ngd-head-tags:\n  - >-\n    `<script type=\"application/ld+json\">[{\"@context\": \"https://schema.org\",\n    \"@type\": \"SoftwareSourceCode\", \"name\": \"EdgeCase\", \"description\":\n    \"High-performance graph analysis library\", \"codeRepository\":\n    \"https://github.com/rich-iannone/edgecase\", \"license\":\n    \"https://opensource.org/licenses/MIT\", \"url\":\n    \"https://rich-iannone.github.io/edgecase\", \"documentation\":\n    \"https://rich-iannone.github.io/edgecase\", \"programmingLanguage\": {\"@type\":\n    \"ComputerLanguage\", \"name\": \"Python\"}}, {\"@context\": \"https://schema.org\",\n    \"@type\": \"WebPage\", \"name\": \"Graph.add_gnc_graph - EdgeCase\", \"isPartOf\":\n    {\"@type\": \"WebSite\", \"name\": \"EdgeCase\"}, \"url\":\n    \"https://rich-iannone.github.io/edgecase/reference/Graph.add_gnc_graph.html\"}]</script>`{=html}\nopen-graph:\n  title: >-\n    [Graph.add_gnc_graph()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function .doc-label\n    .doc-label-method} | EdgeCase\n  description: >-\n    Add a growing network with copying (GNC): each new node links to a random\n    node and to\ntwitter-card:\n  title: >-\n    [Graph.add_gnc_graph()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function .doc-label\n    .doc-label-method} | EdgeCase\n  description: >-\n    Add a growing network with copying (GNC): each new node links to a random\n    node and to\ntitle: >-\n  [Graph.add_gnc_graph()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function .doc-label\n  .doc-label-method}\nbody-classes: doc-api-page doc-py-api-page\nshift-heading-level-by: 0\nbread-crumbs: false\ntoc-depth: 3\ndescription-meta: >-\n  Add a growing network with copying (GNC): each new node links to a random node\n  and to\npage-navigation: false\nhtml-table-processing: none\n---\n\n::: {.doc-subject}\n\nAdd a growing network with copying (GNC): each new node links to a random node and to\n:::\n\n::: {.doc-usage-source}\nUsage\n\n[Source](https://github.com/rich-iannone/edgecase/blob/main/python/edgecase/graph.py#L14650-L14778){target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"}\n:::\n\n::: {.doc-signature .doc-function}\n```pythonsignature\nGraph.add_gnc_graph(\n    n,\n    seed=None,\n    type=None,\n    label=True,\n    rel=None,\n)\n```\n:::\n\n::: {.doc-text}\neverything it links to.\n\n![Growing network with copying](../assets/graphs/add-gnc-graph.light.svg){.lightbox fig-alt=\"Three growing networks with copying on 4, 5, and 6 nodes, placed on a ring in arrival order. Each new node links to a random existing node and also copies all of that node's arcs, so arcs pile onto the earliest nodes: the first node receives 3, 4, and 5 arcs, and the graphs have 4, 7, and 11 arcs in all.\" width=\"100%\"}\n\nAdds nodes one at a time. Each new node picks an existing node uniformly at random and\nlinks to it and to every node it links to. Since those links were copied the same way,\na node's out-neighbors are exactly the nodes on the path from it back to the first\nnode in the underlying random tree. The result is a directed acyclic graph that is\ntransitively closed: whenever `a -> b -> c`, also `a -> c`. It models a paper that\ncites another along with that one's references. The model is due to Krapivsky and\nRedner; the process follows NetworkX's `gnc_graph()`.\n\nEach edge points from the new node to an older one. In an undirected graph the same\nedges are added without their direction. The graph is appended without touching or\nconnecting to existing nodes, and the whole construction is recorded as a single step.\n\nGNC graphs are useful for:\n\n- modeling citation networks in which references are copied\n- random transitively closed DAGs, e.g. as partial orders\n- comparing against `add_gnr_graph()`, which copies a single link instead of all of them\n:::\n\n## Parameters {.doc-parameters}\n\n::: {.doc-definition-items}\n<code>[n]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [int]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>\n\n:   Number of nodes.\n\n<code>[seed]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [Optional[int]]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [None]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>\n\n:   Integer seed for the random number generator. The same `seed=` with the same other\n    arguments always gives the same graph. If `None` (default), the graph differs on\n    every call.\n\n<code>[type]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [Optional[str]]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [None]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>\n\n:   Optional value stored in the `type` attribute of every new node. If `None` (default),\n    no `type` attribute is set.\n\n<code>[label]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [bool]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [[True]{.va}]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>\n\n:   If `True` (default), each new node is labelled with its node ID as a string; a label\n    that is already used raises `ValueError` before anything is added. If `False`, the\n    nodes are left unlabelled.\n\n<code>[rel]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [Optional[str]]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [None]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>\n\n:   Optional value stored in the `rel` attribute of every new edge. If `None` (default),\n    no `rel` attribute is set.\n:::\n\n## Returns {.doc-returns}\n\n::: {.doc-definition-items}\n<code> [Graph]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>\n\n:   Self for method chaining. The graph is modified in place.\n:::\n\n## Raises {.doc-raises}\n\n::: {.doc-definition-items}\n<code> [ValueError]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>\n\n:   If `n=` is negative.\n:::\n\n## See Also {.doc-see-also}\n\n[](`~add_gn_graph`) \n\n:   Grow a tree in which each new node adds one link.\n\n[](`~add_gnr_graph`) \n\n:   Grow a tree by redirecting links.\n\n[](`~add_partial_duplication_graph`) \n\n:   Grow an undirected graph by copying neighborhoods.\n\n## Examples {.doc-examples}\n\nEach new node links to a random existing node and also copies all of that node's links,\nso the graph is much denser than a tree:\n\n::: {#171be2cc .cell execution_count=1}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\nimport edgecase as ec\n\ngraph = ec.Graph(directed=True).add_gnc_graph(n=50, seed=2)\n\ngraph.count_nodes(), graph.count_edges()\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=1}\n```\n(50, 137)\n```\n:::\n:::\n\n\nBecause the links are copied, every node's successors include all of its successors'\nsuccessors:\n\n::: {#4ae826e3 .cell execution_count=2}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\nall(\n    set(graph.get_successors(w)) <= set(graph.get_successors(v))\n    for v in graph.get_node_ids()\n    for w in graph.get_successors(v)\n)\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=2}\n```\nTrue\n```\n:::\n:::\n\n\n### Copying links or not\n\nTwelve nodes grown with copying on the left, and on the right `add_gn_graph()`, where\neach new node adds a single link. Copying always includes the link to the first node, so\non the left every other node points to `\"1\"`, and there are `20` edges rather than `11`:\n\n::: {#233683be .cell execution_count=3}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\ncopied = ec.Graph(directed=True).add_gnc_graph(n=12, seed=2)\ntree = ec.Graph(directed=True).add_gn_graph(n=12, seed=2)\n\ncopied.get_in_degree(\"1\"), copied.count_edges(), tree.count_edges()\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=3}\n```\n(11, 20, 11)\n```\n:::\n:::\n\n\n::: {layout-ncol=\"2\"}\n\n::: {#78b7cf37 .cell execution_count=4}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\ncopied.plot(\n    label_by=\"label\", width=320, height=320, legend=False, seed=1\n)\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=4}\n```{=html}\n<div class=\"ec-theme\" style=\"display:contents\"><style class=\"ec-theme-vars\">:where(.ec-theme){--ect-3239c383:#fafafa;--ect-e5dfd507:#94a3b8;--ect-8a2de1d4:#374151;--ect-57425a7e:rgba(15,23,42,0.88);--ect-21ade003: